\pard\plain \sa180\sb180\ltrch\loch {I was right there with Lucy, pushing past the heavy coats and hearing the crunch of snow under my shoes. I could almost see the lamppost glowing in the dark forest, even though I was really just under the oak tree by the fence. Dad had bought me the book before he left for work overseas\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 he said it was one of his favorites when he was my age. }
\par\plain {I wondered if he ever wished the wardrobe was real, too. Maybe he had even tried to find one when he was a kid. I thought about the old wardrobe in Grandma\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s attic, the one that only smelled like mothballs and dust. Still\u8230\'85 }
\par\plain {What if there really was a door? A secret place nobody had found yet. Maybe even here, hidden in plain sight.}
\par\plain {Then something bounced against my shoe.}
\par\plain {I looked up. The ball. It had rolled all the way to me, still wobbling a little in the grass.}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Oi, Joey!\u8221\'94 someone yelled. \u8220\'93Can you pass it over?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {I shut the book carefully, keeping my thumb on the page so I wouldn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t lose my place. I stood up and gave the ball a kick. Too hard. It flew over their heads and thumped down into the ditch.}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Aw man, Joey!\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Sorry,\u8221\'94 I said, my face going hot.}
\par\plain {One of the boys jogged toward me, grinning. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Aren\u8217\'92t you gonna play with us? We\u8217\'92re missing one more.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {I hugged the book tighter under my arm. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Um\u8230\'85 no thanks. I\u8217\'92ll just watch.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Okay then. See ya, Joey.\u8221\'94 He was already running back before I finished.}
\par\plain {I sat down again and opened the book where my thumb had been. The game started up like nothing had happened, all shouting and laughter. I wasn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t in it. But the snow in Narnia was waiting, and that felt better than running around, anyway.}}